dict(d=D, c="ivx synth paranoid plus suicide", m="say", l=3, hard=True,
q="A paranoid man, suspicious of you, admits to “dark thoughts.” You need a valid suicide assessment without triggering more suspicion. Which combination is best?",
a="Explain plainly why you are asking, sit beside rather than across, use behavioral incidents about recent days, and avoid interpretations of his suspicion",
ae="This integrates MacKinnon’s transparency with paranoid patients, Havens’ side-by-side positioning, and Shea’s behavioral incidents (concrete events are harder to distort and less threatening than judgments). Interpreting suspicion early would increase threat.",
w=[("Interpret his suspicion as fear of his own anger, then ask about suicide",
"Early interpretation is felt as attack by paranoid patients and closes the door to honest disclosure."),
("Use warm reassurance to build trust, then ask about suicide in the next session",
"Excess warmth is distrusted, and delaying suicide assessment when “dark thoughts” are mentioned is unsafe."),
("Use a structured rating scale so the process feels objective",
"A scale can help, but forms can feel like surveillance to a paranoid patient. The relational stance matters more.")],
x="Expert interviewing is integrative: different schools offer tools for different problems in the same moment. Here: content tool (CASE/behavioral incidents), relational tool (transparency, counter-projective positioning), and a restraint (no premature interpretation). Naming which tool you are using, and why, is the hallmark of mastery.",
s="Shea, The Practical Art of Suicide Assessment; Havens, Making Contact; MacKinnon, Michels & Buckley",
h="Paranoid + suicide: “Glass walls, side by side, yesterday’s facts.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx synth idealization plus structure", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="A patient tells you in the first 10 minutes that you are wonderful and that all previous therapists were cruel. Her self-description is vague and contradictory. Which formulation integrates these observations best?",
a="Splitting with identity diffusion, suggesting borderline organization, and predicting rapid devaluation of you",
ae="Kernberg’s structural criteria: identity diffusion (vague, contradictory self-description) and primitive defenses (splitting into all-good and all-bad). The countertransference may be flattery or unease (Racker’s complementary identification with an idealized object). Gunderson’s GPM suggests predicting disappointment early.",
w=[("Histrionic personality style at the neurotic level, with intact identity",
"Histrionic features may be present, but contradictory, vague self-descriptions argue against integrated identity."),
("A secure attachment with healthy enthusiasm about a new therapist",
"All-good/all-bad splitting with identity diffusion is not consistent with security."),
("Mania with grandiosity",
"Idealizing the clinician and devaluing others is not mania.")],
x="Good formulation draws together observations from different levels: content (what is said), structure (how self and others are represented), process (what happens between you), and countertransference (what you feel). Each confirms or questions the others.",
s="Kernberg, Severe Personality Disorders; Gunderson & Links, BPD: A Clinical Guide",
h="Idealization + vague self = “a spotlight with no stage beneath it.” Expect the light to swing."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx synth mi plus psychodynamic", m="say", l=3, hard=True,
q="A man who drinks heavily says, “My father was a drunk, and I swore I’d never be like him — but a few beers is nothing.” Which response integrates MI with a psychodynamic sensitivity?",
a="“It matters a lot to you not to become like your father, and at the same time a few beers feels like nothing to worry about.”",
ae="This double-sided reflection evokes the discrepancy (MI) between his values and behavior, while respecting the emotional weight of his identification with and against his father (psychodynamic). It does not interpret, push or label.",
w=[("“You may be repeating your father’s pattern without realizing it.”",
"This is an interpretation offered too early; it may provoke defensiveness."),
("“How many beers exactly do you drink each day?”",
"Quantifying is important later but misses the emotional opening."),
("“Your drinking is clearly a problem given your family history.”",
"This is the righting reflex and labeling, which elicit sustain talk.")],
x="Integration means using MI techniques to explore material with dynamic meaning, letting the patient discover the link. Later, he may say himself, “Maybe I’m more like him than I thought.” A self-discovered insight is far more powerful than an interpretation.",
s="Miller & Rollnick, Motivational Interviewing; Malan, Individual Psychotherapy",
h="Double-sided reflection = “hold both hands of the ambivalence.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx synth self disorder vs dissociation", m="ask", l=3, hard=True,
q="A 20-year-old says she sometimes feels she is “not really there” and her thoughts feel “anonymous.” How can you best distinguish schizophrenia-spectrum self-disorder from dissociation?",
a="Explore the structure of experience: anonymous, ownerless thoughts and diminished self-presence suggest self-disorder; detachment linked to stress or trauma with intact ownership suggests dissociation",
ae="EASE describes self-disorders such as thought anonymization (thoughts seem to lose their sense of mineness) and diminished presence, which are persistent and tied to the schizophrenia spectrum. Dissociation (depersonalization) involves feeling detached or unreal, often stress-linked, with preserved sense that thoughts are one’s own.",
w=[("Ask if she has experienced trauma; if yes, it is dissociation",
"Trauma is common in both groups. The structure of experience discriminates better than trauma history."),
("Use a dissociation questionnaire; a high score rules out schizophrenia",
"High dissociation scores occur in schizophrenia too. Questionnaires do not resolve the distinction."),
("Ask if she hears voices; if not, it is dissociation",
"Self-disorders often precede hallucinations. Absence of voices does not exclude the spectrum.")],
x="This is one of the hardest distinctions in early psychosis assessment. Phenomenological interviewing (EASE) gives the finest tools. Combine with course (persistent vs episodic), triggers, functioning and family history.",
s="Parnas et al., EASE (2005); Sass, Schizophr Bull 2014",
h="Self-disorder: “The thought has no owner.” Dissociation: “The owner left the room.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx synth when to switch stance", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="In a first interview, a patient begins describing a vivid traumatic memory, becomes pale, stares, and stops responding. What should you do?",
a="Shift from exploration to grounding: orient her to the present, lower the pace, and postpone detailed trauma exploration",
ae="These signs suggest dissociation or anxiety above the window of tolerance (ISTDP’s cognitive-perceptual disruption, trauma-informed care’s hyperarousal or hypoarousal). Continuing exploration risks retraumatization. Grounding (naming the present place and time, sensory focus) restores regulation.",
w=[("Continue gently, as completing the narrative is therapeutic",
"Continuing when the patient is outside the window of tolerance is not therapeutic."),
("Leave her alone in silence until she recovers",
"Silence can deepen dissociation. Active grounding is needed."),
("End the interview immediately and refer for trauma therapy",
"Abrupt ending can feel like abandonment. Ground first, then plan.")],
x="Siegel’s window of tolerance, Ogden’s sensorimotor work and Abbass’ anxiety pathways converge: you track the body and adjust the interview. Mastery includes knowing when to stop asking.",
s="Siegel, The Developing Mind; Abbass, Reaching Through Resistance",
h="Window of tolerance: “If they leave the window, help them back in before you open new doors.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx synth questions as interventions", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="Systemic therapists (Tomm), MI practitioners and solution-focused therapists all share an assumption about questions. What is it?",
a="Questions are never neutral data gathering; they shape what the person notices, remembers and believes, and so are interventions in themselves",
ae="Tomm wrote of interventive interviewing; MI uses questions to evoke change talk; SFBT uses questions to construct solutions; Loftus showed questions shape memory. Every question selects and frames.",
w=[("Questions should be purely neutral to avoid influencing the patient",
"Perfect neutrality is impossible. The skill is choosing the influence deliberately and ethically."),
("Questions are less important than interpretations",
"These traditions see questions as powerful interventions."),
("Only closed questions influence patients",
"Open questions also influence by what they invite.")],
x="This principle could be considered a unifying idea in a new paradigm of interviewing: the interviewer is always intervening. The diagnostic interview and the therapeutic interview are not separable. Expert interviewers choose questions that gather data and help at the same time.",
s="Tomm, Family Process 1987–1988; Miller & Rollnick; de Shazer",
h="Every question is a small intervention: “You don’t just find the answer, you shape it.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx synth psychotic plus countertransference", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="While interviewing a patient with chronic schizophrenia, you feel strangely disconnected and confused, and lose track of your own questions. What is the most useful way to understand this?",
a="Your confusion may reflect the patient’s disorganized inner state communicated through projective identification, as well as the effect of thought disorder on you",
ae="Searles and Bion described how psychotic patients can induce confusion and fragmentation in the therapist. Understanding this as data, rather than personal incompetence, lets you regain balance and possibly name the patient’s experience (“It may feel very confusing inside right now”).",
w=[("You are tired and should take a break",
"Fatigue is possible, but the specific confusion is meaningful."),
("You lack experience and should refer the patient",
"Experienced clinicians also experience this."),
("The patient is deliberately trying to confuse you",
"This is usually not deliberate.")],
x="Searles’ paper “The effort to drive the other person crazy” describes how relational patterns can produce confusion. Rümke’s praecox feeling might also partly reflect this induced experience. Use it carefully as one piece of data.",
s="Searles, Collected Papers on Schizophrenia; Bion, Second Thoughts",
h="Induced confusion: “Their fog drifts into your room.” Name the fog, don’t get lost in it."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx synth chief complaint vs real request", m="ask", l=3, hard=True,
q="A woman requests “something for sleep.” During the interview, she mentions her husband’s drinking three times, then laughs it off. Integrating MacKinnon, Shea and Sullivan, what is the best next step?",
a="Use a referred gate to return to the husband’s drinking, gently exploring safety and its effect on her, while still addressing sleep",
ae="Repeated mentions signal importance (MacKinnon’s ticket of admission vs real problem). Laughing it off may be a security operation (Sullivan). A referred gate (Shea) uses her own words to enter the topic naturally. Safety must be assessed (alcohol and domestic violence).",
w=[("Prescribe a hypnotic, as she requested",
"Prescribing without exploring the underlying problem misses the likely cause and possible safety issues."),
("Confront her about avoiding the topic of her husband",
"Confrontation may increase defensiveness. A gentle referred gate is more effective."),
("Ignore the mentions, as she laughed them off",
"Ignoring repeated mentions misses the real request.")],
x="The ticket of admission concept is central in primary care and psychiatry: people often present an acceptable complaint (sleep) to test whether the clinician can be trusted with a harder one. Track repeated mentions and laughter as clues.",
s="MacKinnon, Michels & Buckley; Shea; Sullivan",
h="Ticket of admission: “Insomnia is the ticket; the husband is the show.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx synth cultural idiom vs psychosis", m="ask", l=3, hard=True,
q="A Lebanese woman says she has been hit by the evil eye (al-ʿayn) since a neighbor praised her new house, and since then she has been unable to sleep. Which approach integrates phenomenology and cultural formulation?",
a="Explore whether the belief is shared within her community and fits a recognized explanatory model, while assessing form, fixity and associated symptoms",
ae="Culturally shared beliefs are not delusions (DSM excludes beliefs accepted by the person’s culture or subculture). The CFI explores meaning; phenomenology examines form (is there delusional perception, unshakeable conviction beyond the cultural norm, other psychotic symptoms?).",
w=[("Diagnose a persecutory delusion because the belief is unscientific",
"This ignores cultural context and risks misdiagnosis."),
("Accept the belief and do not assess further",
"Cultural acceptance does not exclude co-occurring psychiatric illness; sleep disturbance needs assessment."),
("Ask a family member to confirm whether she is psychotic",
"Collateral can help but does not replace careful assessment.")],
x="Ask: “What do others in your family think? What does it mean? What helps?” and assess for depression, anxiety and other symptoms. Integrating explanatory models into treatment (e.g., combining religious practices with medical care) improves engagement.",
s="DSM-5-TR Cultural Formulation Interview; Kleinman (1978); Jaspers",
h="Cultural belief vs delusion: “Shared by the village or held alone against it?”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx synth adolescent self harm", m="say", l=3, hard=True,
q="A 15-year-old shows you healing cuts and says, “Don’t tell my parents.” Which response integrates adolescent interviewing, trauma-informed care and risk assessment?",
a="Thank her for showing you, explain honestly what you must share for safety and what remains confidential, and explore the function and risk of the self-harm",
ae="HEEADSSS principles: confidentiality with clear limits. Trauma-informed: honesty and collaboration. Risk: explore suicidal intent, method, frequency, and function (e.g., emotion regulation). Promising total secrecy is dishonest; disclosing without discussion damages trust.",
w=[("Promise not to tell her parents to keep her trust",
"Promising secrecy you cannot keep undermines trust later."),
("Call her parents immediately while she waits",
"Acting without discussing it with her damages the relationship and her sense of control."),
("Tell her self-harm is dangerous and she must stop",
"Lecturing does not reduce self-harm and may increase shame.")],
x="Involve the adolescent in decisions about how parents will be informed (who says what, when). Explore functions (relief of tension, punishment, communication), and assess suicidality separately, since non-suicidal self-injury and suicidal behavior can coexist.",
s="Goldenring & Rosen (HEEADSSS); NICE guidance on self-harm (2022)",
h="Adolescent confidentiality: “Honest limits beat broken promises.”"),

